rejector: requests go out in input order, so replies pair with their rows (checkpoint 1)

DRAFT for rejector v1. Not applied, not judged, uncommitted; the user edits the wording.

The benchmark's mistake, stated late. The mock API hands out queued replies first come, first
served. test_first_number_extract (checkpoint 2) queues "an 8 out of 10" then "Score: 7.5" for two
rows and wants row one to score 8; the failing runs' row one got 7.5, the second reply. 5 pass,
4 fail, and not the same runs twice: a race. Checkpoint 1 requires concurrency ("The tool must
issue requests concurrently") and says nothing about order of dispatch. Checkpoint 5 does:
"rows are still paired with outputs in input order even when requests overlap; concurrent
execution must not cause a later input row to consume the response intended for an earlier row".
That is the rule the checkpoint-2 test needs, three checkpoints late, and a checkpoint's agent
sees only its own spec. No registry carries the question. The sentence below borrows checkpoint
5's words for the section where the dispatcher is written. It narrows the race and cannot close
it: with a first-come mock, two requests sent a millisecond apart can still arrive swapped, so
the test stays flaky by design (upstream: key the mock's replies by prompt, not by arrival).

